  <section class="content-block">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="text-center section-title">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-section">
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How many sports can you fit on one court?</h3>
          <p>This depends on court size and sport compatibility. A full basketball court can accommodate four pickleball courts, tennis and pickleball can share space efficiently, and most courts can handle 2-4 sports with proper line configuration and equipment planning.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Do different sports interfere with each other?</h3>
          <p>With proper design, sports can coexist beautifully. We use color-coded line systems, strategic equipment placement, and clear visual separation to ensure each sport maintains its integrity and playability within the shared space.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Are multi-sport courts more expensive than single-sport courts?</h3>
          <p>Multi-sport courts typically cost slightly more due to additional line marking and equipment, but they provide significantly more value per square foot. The cost per sport decreases dramatically compared to building separate courts for each activity.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How do you handle different net heights for different sports?</h3>
          <p>We install versatile net systems that can be quickly adjusted for different sports, or provide separate net posts for sports requiring different heights. Portable and semi-permanent solutions are both available depending on your usage patterns.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Can I add sports to an existing court later?</h3>
          <p>In most cases, yes! Existing courts can often accommodate additional sports with line marking, equipment installation, and minor modifications. We evaluate existing surfaces and provide recommendations for expansion possibilities.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

